inline.s:
Upstream I follow the far line of shoals;
My journey moors beside a lonely pavilion.
The moon withdrawn, the mountain's face grows pale;
The fish dive deep, the water's breath is rank.
Grass-insects chirr along the broken bank;
Sand-birds roost on the cold and barren isle.
Most do I pity him who drifts and falls —
This floating life, its dream not yet awake.

A half couch serves the stillness of my Chan;
On hand-propped cheek I lie in white cloud air.
Its humble, hollow heart dotes on me still;
Its lofty joints — for you alone I care.
Wiped clean, it keeps the Xiang queens' tearful stains;
Cut fine, it weeps the patterns of old Chu.
Best it can soothe the sickness of my bones;
From this day forth I bar all worldly hue.

Through many wars this body has grown old;
In wind and smoke my temples show their gray.
My bones are tired and shun the iron mail;
My heart grown cold, I love the green hills' way.
On wooden slips, the taste of Chan, detached;
In tea's sweet fragrance all affairs grow still.
Gladly with the white clouds I make my home —
Who would let me pass the pine-gate sill?

A single stream winds inward to the vale;
The crowding peaks turn back on either shore.
One thinks the folk still live as in Qin's day;
The monks seem come from India's ancient shore.
Bamboo and trees grow lengthening into cloud;
The fields unfold as spreads the land below.
Who knows, within the Five Ranges' winding turns,
A Tiantai of its own is set to show?

A hundred feet from one small sprout arise;
A single root thrusts up through weeds and brush.
It has endured the smoke and miasma's grief;
Has heard the crane's lament cry out and hush.
Put to great use, it should not come to harm;
Cut to a shape, how should that be a woe?
I only grieve for tonight's moon, that shines
In vain upon the mosses here below.

A fragrant shrine floats on the hidden sea;
A flowered banner lifts a Brahma hall.
Green spiral shows the Buddha's jeweled hair;
The full moon shows his kindly face to all.
The world lies level as a palm outspread;
The river's current cleans to empty space.
Pity the hunted ones who run and strive:
They all fall within the dharmakāya's grace.

Upon this wild road near and far are one;
Its windings double every stage I go.
The land turns with the river as it runs;
Men walk a path that only birds can know.
A lone duck vanishes among the clouds;
A single sail hangs light at treetops' ends.
I turn my head toward the road of Chang'an:
No frontier goose-song to my ear descends.

I urge my horse and watch the post-house near;
This long road I have traveled many a year.
All day the mountain vapors drift in white;
In the tenth month the burned ground greens aright.
My face burns hot with betel-nut's hot wine;
My spirit dulls in sea-fog's rank and brine.
From the lone town the horn and drum arise —
So grim, so grand, I cannot bear their cries.

The waning moon hangs on the city wall;
The bugle deepens a traveler's sorrow.
The north wind blows about my thinning hair;
Cold dew soaks through my heavy fur.
Wild burnings run along the ramparts' line;
Frontier beacons dim the watchtower's face.
A lonely cloud stands pale, then drifts away —
At ease at last, floating in empty space.

Ridges coil away across the vastness;
Their windings leave no count of fold or tier.
The road on either hand seems Hangu Pass;
The people seem of Wuling's hidden mere.
Deep in the woods the tigers and leopards hide;
Far in the sky the falcon stoops to strike.
And why upon this windy, dusty way
Must I, an old monk, drive and urge all day?

Alone I travel beyond ten thousand li;
The farther the road, the deeper grows my sigh.
The hills wear all the vapor of the python;
The speech of men is birds' strange chattering.
A wayside hut is all today's affair;
Ice and snow, the heart of a whole life.
Though trees that pierce the sky are growing here,
They are no Jeta Grove, to saints most dear.

I pass out through a barren valley's mouth;
Above the treetops see the world's far end.
The wild is wide and keeps the lingering light;
The ruined town is girt with sunset's red.
Urged on by hunger, I forget my weariness;
To hurry more, I count the long road still.
At dusk I lodge within a mountain inn —
I sleep as soundly as at home, at will.

The hills bow low and plunge into the gorge;
A single stream comes winding to the village.
The level plain unfolds the burning frontier;
Along the border garrison camps lie.
The people's lives waste all their years in vain;
The seasons lose their cold, their warmth forget.
Say not the world's end lies too far away:
Fusang is near the dawn of coming day.

The long road wearies all the strength of foot;
The broad plain opens out before my eyes.
The land here ends at the Southern Sea;
Pearl luster links the Dipper in the skies.
Far hills lie low and merge into the trees;
The great sea stands level with the sky.
It looks like clouds with drooping wings outspread —
Sails spread, the swift treasure-ship goes by.

In this hot land the seasons strange appear;
The year's affairs are hard to reckon clear.
The twelfth month ends, yet insects do not hide;
Spring comes, and birds know nothing it has come.
Bean-blossoms open on the old bean-pods;
Banyan leaves fall from the newest bough.
And so I think of Yan Mountain's snows —
The kindly warmth seems partial to them now.

Over the Qiong Sea the spring clouds gather deep;
The steamy heat lies sunk in lingering mist.
The orchid's fragrance turns to fevered air;
The thorny bamboo grows to forests' grief.
Ask nothing of the labors of this life;
Look only at this heart that nears its death.
I tire of beard and hair, this heavy load —
Day after day I long to draw the pin.

Autumn waters brim with lotus flowers;
A little boat, one leaf of slender weight.
On the still stream I am in my old house;
To drift abroad is but to journey home.
A scattered rain drives back the steamy heat;
A clear wind lifts the ripples like crepe's crease.
I come and go wherever I am inclined;
The partridge's “Go not!” I pay no mind.

On either bank the hills let fall their shade;
A thousand peaks pour upside down through space.
Among the clouds flies Vulture Peak away;
Beneath the waves the dragon palace shows.
In fine descent the heavenly flowers rain;
Long sounds the wind, the music of the earth.
The racing current takes my boat in tow —
For one brief while it will not let me go.

Cold rain comes sprinkling on my brushwood door;
The chilly sound by little and little dies.
At first I think it but a lean plum's shade;
I scarce believe that snow can fly at all.
It presses heavy on the plantain leaves;
It lightly mocks the creeping fig's green cloak.
Eight years my dreams have labored toward this sight;
Today I joy to see the falling light.

Bearing my pack through ten thousand li;
Seven years I tarried in the burning land.
I chose my dwelling by the Ganges' bank;
My heart was drawn by one long stream outspanned.
Having a body, I can bear its load;
Having no thing, no thoughts need I compose.
Before the cliff where the cut arm's tree stands,
Once more I breathe the cassia's fragrance close.

The great earth floats upon the fragrant sea;
A lonely spire lifts like a Brahma banner.
In sky and water Vulture Peak appears;
In caves of fire the poison dragon yields.
Sun and moon hang like mirrors in the void;
Heaven and earth glow in a night-lamp's light.
I gaze at clouds and flick my five fingers free —
A rain of flowers falls at the empty window's height.

The general's hall stands cold and desolate;
Here I lodge, a banished guest, and wait.
Too lazy, I let worldly matters go;
More idle than the white clouds, drifting slow.
I wave my whisk, too slack to tame the deer;
Beside the pool I learn the goose-lover's cheer.
In some deep valley, hidden from the day,
Could it be better? That I cannot say.

The white sun burns as fierce as fire can be;
I lie and sleep; the night air chills me through.
Waking from dream, I come back to the moon's cave;
My thoughts go down into an icy bowl.
At the camp gate, drums and horns at dawn;
The River of Stars fades in the morning pale.
Awake, I let my loosened hair hang free —
I grieve to see the bamboo-skin cap on me.

These pond waters bring lake and river thoughts;
The fishes' joy I have not yet forgot.
Forever I remember the great gulf;
My secret mind rests on the Hao bridge's way.
The new moon sinks — a slender, bending hook;
The willow trails a long and silken line.
Tonight the wind and rain will rise and shake —
How the scaled fins will leap and flash and shine!

Easily I put off dust's entanglements;
Hard to dissolve this body of great woe.
In act or rest I mingle with the crowd;
But age and sickness spare no man, I know.
Ox teeth and horse teeth soon will be worn down;
The ape's and monkey's mind is not yet pure.
My six sense-faculties hold their own domains —
I cannot tell which one is kin and sure.

A narrow path, a hillside's slender waist;
The clear stream's ribbon stretches long and low.
The pine-cones fall to meet the facing wind;
Dew-soaked, the rice-bloom sends its fragrance up.
Village roofs are green as lotus buds;
The houses stand with walls of whitened board.
If you have never come to Peach Blossom Spring,
Then ask no fisherman to be your guide.

At dusk I come down from the lofty peak;
Deep in the hills the heat lies thick and still.
The air steams under dog-day sun and burns —
Then coolness sprinkles over a pine-clad hill.
The rushing wind drives on the chilling rain;
Rank-smelling clouds wake dragons where they lie.
I hurry home to lodge in Huazang's walls;
The dream breaks — the upper bell sounds from the sky.

A single reach leads on to the fragrant sea;
A thousand peaks about the phantom city crowd.
The blue lotus unfolds its slender leaves;
The moon of wisdom shines aloft and bright.
The world far off, all conditions come to rest;
The mind at leisure, the five defilements light.
If but one mote of dust may be broke through,
Here and now the unborn is realized.

A sudden rain drives off the burning heat;
The new-come autumn breathes a crisper air.
The bank's sand disappears beneath the flood;
The river moon keeps company with me.
Gathered foam is but the toil of living;
Floating clouds, a passing traveler's heart.
Beside the stream I watch the bubbles' gleam —
And see this body turn to something light.

One leaf goes riding off upon the wind;
The little boat goes homeward with the stream.
The hills coil round like ants upon the ground;
The river winds about me like a ring.
This body merges with the empty clouds;
The mind lies idle with the moon on the water.
Ten thousand peaks — I lie down safe at home;
In silence I close up the pine-wood gate.

The setting sun is lovelier when it clears;
The homeward road grows cold and longer drawn.
An idle heart finds clouds that never weary;
The birds must know the tiredness I bear.
The roads of the world will never find an end;
This toiling life must have a term somewhere.
I turn my head among ten thousand peaks:
Who chews the purple-stemmed herb on those steeps?

An empty boat goes where it will go;
One stretch of water ends the passes' woe.
The moon looks lovelier the more I gaze;
The river's voice grows quieter as I hear.
Floating clouds are matters outside the self;
White hair is but the mirror's image here.
Say not that I have drifted far and long;
The way ahead is my own mountain's song.

Not tarrying, I am a guest from the first;
An empty boat, a tumbleweed that rolls.
On either side the river, cliffs a thousand feet;
Half sail in rain, and half a sail of wind.
It skims the rocks as swallows skim the air;
It rides the current as on emptiness.
The ancient meaning of the ferry lost —
It lies in every coming and every going.

Drop after drop, the heart has lost its thread;
Thread after thread, the mood grows skilled in pain.
A single boat is lost by distant shores;
Two eyes grow dim across the long, wet sky.
The trees of a thousand villages are gone;
Still blows the wind through ten thousand hollow places.
Grief adds itself to the newly gathered waters —
Rolling and rolling in the rushing stream.

In old age I would lean on quiet grace;
My truest hope a grove where Dharma grows.
A hundred years are gathered in tonight's talk;
Kalpas on kalpas meet in this hour's mind.
The snow lies white across the Hengshan's back;
The clouds lie deep upon the Xiang's dark stream.
To rest and have done — that now is all my aim;
No more will I spend strength on seeking's dream.

A heart that wearies of the world grows fixed;
How shall it bear when sickness plays the fiend?
I know already that my days are few;
And still this body seems the more to grow.
Medicine and stone fill up the fragrant bowl;
My very groaning is my karma-rite.
If Mañjuśrī should come and question me —
One silent pause: what answer could be right?

Spring rain has passed across the Xiao and Xiang;
A light sail hangs in morning's frosty air.
The racing current turns at Stone Drum's height;
The risen water crosses through Hengyang.
The marchmount's color seems to draw quite near;
The lake's far sky stretches away in length.
Who knows that in this sea of dust and pain
A ford, a bridge, are found in every place?

The setting sunlight soaks the lake and sky;
The sand is bright, the moon is in the boat.
Birds roost in trees that lean above the water;
Voices speak beyond the wood's thin smoke.
In this floating world I lodge a single night;
This remnant life — how many years can be?
Why is it, in my lean and fading days,
I am still held by Chu's riverside to stay?

The lake is shallow, and not hard to cross;
Yet the wind's sail is not so lightly set.
The sheep-gut sands wind crooked as they run;
The bird-feather banks lie ragged and uneven.
The land bends round, two turning wheels in flight;
The empty sky hangs like a single mirror.
I look back at the place where I must moor —
The new moon shines on Moth-Eyebrow, and clearer.

The many mountains join the earth's own veins;
The many waters pour to Heaven's Heart.
Vast and measureless, the world is wide;
Sun and moon float and sink in depths apart.
The sail flies with a solitary bird;
My gaze goes out into the level wood.
If I should follow where the small boat goes —
In mist and wave, where could I then be found?

The whitewashed walls are hid in morning glow;
The lonely town stands by the water's side.
Along the dike the willows spread their green;
About the wall the peach trees blossom wide.
Far in the sky a yellow swan takes flight;
The clear stream runs with sand of whitest stone.
I know that Wuling cannot be far off —
At the ferry I see a fisherman's home.

His crown possessed the vajra's diamond eye;
His breast concealed a chestnut burr of thorn.
A single phrase went off like thunder's crack;
From all four seas the heroes came, and ran.
The patriarch's intent — offered before the trigger;
All common feeling — emptied where it stands.
The Chan gate's born slayer of the self —
Awesome, one sees the true wind in his hands.

My hair is not so white as is my heart;
My form is like a withered, leafless tree.
All conditions end in the place of rest;
One single thought, seen through, is left alone.
Heaven already pardons my crude slowness;
True Chan is free of being and of non-being.
My remnant life is like the setting sun —
And every step I take is homeward road.

This body is no longer safe to trust;
Its broken parts each have their reason why.
For a while the four great elements join;
At last they gather into specks of dust.
Ten thousand sounds hold emptiness and quiet;
All conditions show the original truth.
Always and always, amid sound and form,
How many people have been led astray.

A hut no bigger than a bushel measure —
Within it I let roll and unroll at pleasure.
Clouds and bright mists rise from door and window;
Stars and the moon hang on the courtyard stair.
Thoughts stilled, the mind grows quieter and quieter;
Dust gone, the world of things is as it is.
The southern breeze comes sometimes to my seat;
Rustling, the six windows stand empty and clear.

When I have eaten, I have no other task;
I sleep so long I know not day from night.
Of dark and light I scarcely am aware;
How often have I heard the bells and drums?
On every side blue peaks are curtains hung;
Together with my body I lie in white cloud.
Who says that tea has power and vigor in it,
Able to rout the armies of sleep's god?

I have no heart for this world of men;
My spirit roams in the Heaven of Utmost Bliss.
There stays but this old vessel full of leaks;
I am ashamed to deal in blind-man's Chan.
I envy only wings that beat the wind;
I prize the lotus rising from the water.
I look back at the guests who dwell in dust —
Who is not tangled, sleeping, and asleep?

The Xiang's waters run to Ba and Han;
A lonely sail goes into Chu's wide sky.
A patch of cloud lies low on distant trees;
The clear sun shines upon the slanting stream.
To live in this world is always to be a guest;
Of this floating life, ask not how many years.
Even if I follow the road that leads me home,
It is still like a boat that waits at the ferry.

At dawn I leave the clear pool's winding bend;
I raise my sail and trust the water's flow.
The sail goes flying after birds in flight;
The banks turn with me as the boat moves on.
The trees are far — one thinks the sky ends there;
The river's empty — see the earth afloat.
Lake Dongting lies, it seems, a foot away;
And step by step I near Yueyang's tower.

The boat lies moored among the reeds for the night;
At break of day I pass by Jiayu's wall.
The hills come out — the town looks small above;
The river holds the trees' sparse shadows all.
The sky hangs low — I think the sun is near;
The water reaches far — as on the void I ride.
A single reed goes off upon the wind,
Drifting wherever winds and waters guide.

Seeking out beauty, I traveled Xiao and Xiang;
Riding the current, I passed by Wuchang.
Rivers and hills make Hankou's grandeur stand;
Clouds and rain once deceived the King of Xiang.
Far off, the tracks of the flying yellow crane;
A light sail hangs against the setting sun.
This life goes drifting with the departing water —
No need to ask if I should stay or run.
